"{\"url\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/project/prj_1K48633C61S0WY58\",\"title\":\"CarbonZero.eco - Williams — Isometric\",\"head\":[\"UNDER VALIDATION\",\"BICRS\",\"CarbonZero.eco - Williams\",\"CarbonZero\",\"->\",\"PROJECT DETAILS\",\"Expected first certificates\",\"2027\",\"Project code\",\"Z9PR\",\"Crediting period\",\"01 Jul 2026 to 31 Jul 2031\"],\"issued_header\":null,\"buffer_header\":null,\"crediting\":null,\"verifier\":null,\"code\":\"Z9PR\",\"approval\":\"Expected 2027\",\"protocol_current\":\"Biochar Production and Storage v1.3\",\"protocol_baseline\":null,\"cert_issued\":null,\"cert_retired\":null,\"cert_buffer\":null,\"reversal\":null,\"showing\":[\"Showing 2 of 2\"],\"tables\":[{\"heads\":[\"Submission date\",\"Title\",\"Download\"],\"rows\":[[\"09 Apr 2026\",\"Public Comment Summary\",\"PDF | ->\"],[\"10 Mar 2026\",\"PDD\",\"PDF | ->\"]]}],\"links\":[{\"h\":\"https://carbonzero.eco\",\"t\":\"CarbonZero\"},{\"h\":\"/protocol/biochar/1.3\",\"t\":\"Biochar Production and Storage v1.3\"},{\"h\":\"/supplier/spl_1K473ARXN1S0D5MQ\",\"t\":\"Learn more\"},{\"h\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/standard#validation-and-verification-requirements\",\"t\":\"Isometric Standard\"},{\"h\":\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KOmLuhU6yzCYYtUWPyQCw4aByEEJ7Q1-/view\",\"t\":\"VVB Policy\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1K473ARXN1S0D5MQ/prj_1K48633C61S0WY58/pjd_1KNRTW1VB1S0DM0R/public_comment_summary.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1K473ARXN1S0D5MQ/prj_1K48633C61S0WY58/pjd_1KKBMXMC11S0KDSN/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"UNDER VALIDATION\\nBICRS\\nCarbonZero.eco - Williams\\nCarbonZero\\n->\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nExpected first certificates\\n2027\\nProject code\\nZ9PR\\nCrediting period\\n01 Jul 2026 to 31 Jul 2031\\nPROTOCOL DETAILS\\nCurrent protocol version\\nBiochar Production and Storage v1.3\\nSummary\\n\\nThe Williams Biochar Facility in Colusa County converts almond shells from 300+ farms into 10,000 tonnes of biochar annually, generating ~20,000 carbon removal credits. Upon expansion, the project will scale up to over 108,000 carbon removal credits per year. Applied locally, the biochar boosts soil fertility, water retention, and crop yields while reducing fertilizer use, transport emissions, and locking carbon in a durable, circular system.\\n\\nColusa County, CA\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nBiomass Carbon Removal and Storage (BiCRS) involves storing the carbon contained in waste biomass—organic material from living or recently living organisms. BiCRS works by halting or slowing the decomposition of biomass, enabling the carbon it contains to be stored long-term. This can be achieved by converting biomass into materials that decompose more slowly—such as biochar or bio-oil—or by storing it in environments where decomposition is slowed. For BiCRS to count as carbon removal, the biomass must be replenished to ensure additional carbon dioxide is drawn from the atmosphere.\\n\\nLearn more\\nProject timeline\\nCompleted 2025\\nInitial documentation submission\\nCompleted 19 Mar 2026\\nPublic comment period\\nExpected 2026\\nIN PROGRESS\\nAudit kick-off\\n\\nA Validation & Verification Body (VVB) officially commences the project validation and verification process. All VVBs are approved and appointed by Isometric based on the requirements outlined in the Isometric Standard and VVB Policy.\\n\\nExpected 2026\\nSite visit\\nExpected 2027\\nApproval date\\nExpected 2027\\nInitial credit issuance\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n09 Apr 2026\\tPublic Comment Summary\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n10 Mar 2026\\tPDD\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 2 of 2\\nPrevious\\nNext\"}"